==English==

===Etymology===
[[backformation|Backformation]] from {{m|en|bludger}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 bludge.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} The act of bludging.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Anne Barry|title=Playing with Fire|page=136|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=jZwaiFghcsMC&pg=PA136&dq=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=uzn4TrzwEI_FmQXw5NjTAg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false|passage=A friend offered him a job working as a handyman in his carpet factory – a Mr Fix-it. Effectively off the '''bludge''' and back on track.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} Easy work.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=Wendy Morgan|title=Critical Literacy in the Classroom: The Art of the Possible|page=145|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=WuirVm1LRkIC&pg=PA145&dq=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=ohz4Tr20NuGfmQXqzL3fBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false|passage=Oh, my name is Gecko and I just thought the whole unit was a '''bludge''', sometimes it got really boring. But like I said I could just fall asleep and let my group members do all the work. And still almost pass.}}
#* '''2011''', Irini Savvides, ''Sky Legs'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=iDIRf4I0CoUC&pg=PT32&dq=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=NlX4TsTLKcb4mAXn7uClAg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false unnumbered page],
#*: ‘Seriously, you′ve got sheep at school?’ I said.
#*: ‘Yeah, heaps of kids here do Ag. Reckon it′s a big '''bludge''', like drama.’

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|easy work}} {{l|en|doddle}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|obsolete|slang}} To live off the earnings of a [[prostitute]].
# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} To not [[earn one's keep]], to live off someone else or off welfare when one could be working.
# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} To [[avoid]] one's [[responsibility|responsibilities]]; to leave it to others to perform duties that one is expected to perform.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Tony Shillitoe|title=Joy Ride|page=64|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=oWJD5EPe6QoC&pg=PA64&dq=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=uzn4TrzwEI_FmQXw5NjTAg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false|passage=The second last Thursday in first term of Year Nine, Jason and I '''bludged''' school for the first time together. It wasn't Jason's first time. He '''bludged''' school regularly, but I never used to miss days unless I was really sick.}}
#* '''2002''', [[w:Donald Friend|Donald Friend]], Anne Gray (editor), ''The Diaries of Donald Friend'', Volume 1, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=phKd3b6kQOwC&pg=PA343&dq=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=GSb4To3MOs2HmQXj3IC2Ag&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false page 343],
#*: One of the mess orderlies had consistently '''bludged''' on the rest of us all day.
# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} To do nothing, to be [[idle]], especially when there is work to be done.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1967|author={{w|New Zealand House of Representatives}}|title=Parliamentary Debates|page=3164|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Y1chAQAAIAAJ&q=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&dq=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=d174Tu2rFuHrmAWL_bGdBA&redir_esc=y|passage=We had the member for Piako saying as recently as last year, when dealing with social security benefits and increases, “I feel myself that when we have able-bodied men and women who would '''bludge''' and draw the pension, there is something wrong.”}}
#* '''1998''', Marion Halligan, Rosanne Fitzgibbon, ''The gift of story: Three decades of UQP short stories'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=qdMgAQAAIAAJ&q=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&dq=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=9Vj4TuudEe_HmQWf3MC0Ag&redir_esc=y page 96],
#*: Now, you get back out there and you '''bludge'''! I don't want to see anyone working, OK? I don't want to see any pick-axes, any hammers, or nothing.
#* '''2004''', John Smyth, Robert Hattam, et al., ''‘Dropping Out,’ Drifting Off, Being Excluded: Becoming Somebody Without School'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=V23j2VOz45AC&pg=PA53&dq=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=GSb4To3MOs2HmQXj3IC2Ag&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22|%22bludges%22|%22bludging%22|%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false page 53],
#*: I mean, school′s like a job. If you work for it you get your grades; if you work your hours you get your money. But if you bludge, you don't get money; if you '''bludge''' you don't get any grades. That's something that I didn't realize when I was young.
# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand|slang}} To take some [[benefit]] and give nothing in return.
#: ''Can I '''bludge''' a cigarette off you?''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1983|author={{w|Max Harris}}|title=The Unknown Great Australian and other psychobiographical portraits|page=105|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=CcXiAAAAMAAJ&q=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&dq=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=ZXT4TtOBIaKhmQWL6KSIAg&redir_esc=y|passage=Gabriel was a classic bludger. He was a drop-out in the very modern sense of the word. The Rossettis were anything but well-heeled. Solid old brother William kept the show on the road. Gabriel '''bludged''' on the family. He '''bludged''' on his mates.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Gillian Cowlishaw|title=Blackfellas, Whitefellas, and the Hidden Injuries of Race|page=135|url=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=F9WwhSCw9wkC&pg=PA135&dq=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22+-intitle:%22bludging%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=GSb4To3MOs2HmQXj3IC2Ag&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bludge%22%7C%22bludges%22%7C%22bludging%22%7C%22bludged%22%20-intitle%3A%22bludging%22&f=false|passage=Now an adult with his own family, this man has become conscious of different norms among his children's white friends, and that whites often see sharing as '''bludging'''.}}

